[{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Functions","page":"Functions","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"function","location":"functions/#GliderAnalysis.gad","page":"Functions","text":"gad(debug::Integer=0, args...)\n\nPrint debugging information, if debug>0. Leading spaces are added in groups of 4, for each integer by which debug exceeds 1. This is called by most functions in this library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GliderAnalysis.gad"},{"category":"function","location":"functions/#GliderAnalysis.lonlat_xy","page":"Functions","text":"lonlat_xy(longitude, latitude; inverse=false, case=:halifax_line, debug=0)\n\nConvert longitude and latitude vectors to x and y vectors (in km), or do the reverse (if inverse=false). The transformation is governed by the case keyword (which has a default value that is useful along the Halifax Line on the Scotian Shelf, off Nova Scotia).\n\nArguments\n\nlongitude vector of longitudes\nlatitude vector of longitudes\n\nKeywords\n\ninverse a Bool value indicating the name of the coordinate conversion. Use inverse=false (the default), to convert longitude,latitude to x,y. Use inverse=true to convert from x,y to longitude,latitude.\ncase either the symbol :halifax_line (which is the default) or a NamedTuple containing the elements longitude0 (the zero of new coordinate system), latitude0 (the zero of new coordinate system), angle (the counterclockwise angle of new coordinate system to an east-north system and srs (the coordinate transformation code). By default, case=(longitude0=-63.507773, latitude0=44.623249, angle=-59.1879, srs=\"EPSG:32620\").  See Reference 1 for information on this default srs value.\ndebug an Integer indicating the degree of printing to be done during processing. Use debug=0 (the default) to work silently, or a higher value to indicate to print some information about the working steps.\n\nReferences\n\nhttps://epsg.io/32620 states the scope of EPSG:32620 to be navigation at medium accuracy, with application to latitudes from the equator to 84°N and longitudes from 66°W to 60°W.\n\nExamples\n\nusing GliderAnalysis, Plots, DataFrames, CSV\n\nfile = joinpath(dirname(dirname(pathof(GliderAnalysis))), \"data\", \"sbloom_2023_traj.csv.gz\")\ntraj = CSV.read(file, DataFrame);\n\nxy = lonlat_xy(traj.longitude, traj.latitude);\n\nKW = (framestyle=:box, tickdirection=:out, label=false, ms=1, guidefontsize=7, tickfontsize=7, titlefontsize=7)\na = Plots.scatter(traj.longitude, traj.latitude, aspect_ratio=1.4,\n    xlab=\"Longitude\", ylab=\"Latitude\", title=file; KW...)\nb = Plots.scatter(xy[:, 1], xy[:, 2], aspect_ratio=1.0,\n    xlab=\"Easting [km]\", ylab=\"Northing [km]\"; KW...)\nPlots.plot(a, b, layout=(1, 2), size=(800, 500))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GliderAnalysis.lonlat_xy"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Transect-lon-lat-to-x-y-calculation","page":"Examples","text":"(Image: Transect coordinate system)\n\nusing GliderAnalysis, Plots, DataFrames, CSV\n\nfile = joinpath(dirname(dirname(pathof(GliderAnalysis))), \"data\", \"sbloom_2023_traj.csv.gz\")\ntraj = CSV.read(file, DataFrame);\n\nxy = lonlat_xy(traj.longitude, traj.latitude)\nll = lonlat_xy(xy[:, 1], xy[:, 2]; inverse=true)\n\nfs = 7\nKW = (framestyle=:box, tickdirection=:out, label=false, ms=1, guidefontsize=fs, tickfontsize=fs, titlefontsize=fs)\na = Plots.scatter(traj.longitude, traj.latitude, aspect_ratio=1.4, xlab=\"Longitude\", ylab=\"Latitude\", title=file; KW...)\nb = Plots.scatter(xy[:, 1], xy[:, 2], aspect_ratio=1.0, xlab=\"Easting [km]\", ylab=\"Northing [km]\", title=\"Using lonlat_xy()\"; KW...)\nPlots.vline!([0], lwd=3, color=:red, label=false)\nPlots.plot(a, b, layout=(1, 2), size=(800, 500))\n\nsavefig(\"lonlat_xy.png\")","title":"Transect lon-lat to x-y calculation"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changes","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"Changes"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changelog-from-version-0.2.12-to-version-0.2.13","page":"Changelog","text":"Release notes:","title":"Changelog from version 0.2.12 to version 0.2.13"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed","page":"Changelog","text":"get_erddap_index(), get_tide_gauge_index(), and get_tide_gauge_metadata() handle non-responsive URLs better, without dumping pages of confusing output from the server.\nBREAKING","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added","page":"Changelog","text":"interpolate_to_time(), used by read_glider() but also perhaps useful for more general purposes, too.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation-methods","page":"Installation","text":"This package is not on the general repository, but it can be installed from its source on GitHub by typing  the following in a Julia console.\n\nusing Pkg ; Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/dankelley/GliderAnalysis.jl\")","title":"Installation methods"},{"category":"section","location":"#GliderAnalysis","page":"Home","text":"GliderAnalysis is a Julia package designed to facilitate the analysis of oceanographic glider data, particularly for a project relating to the Scotian Shelf of eastern Canada.\n\nHome: what you're reading now.\nInstallation: how to install GliderAnalysis.\nFunctions: functions provided in GliderAnalyis.","title":"GliderAnalysis"}]
